# "And for myself": the one idea that would give me a good life in each era (not just help the world).
ME = {
    "1600s": dict(title="Germs are real", text="I'd live by a theory nobody else had yet: invisible living things cause disease. So I'd boil my drinking water (or drink small beer like everyone else), wash with soap, keep wounds clean, and politely refuse the era's two favourite treatments: bloodletting and mercury.",
                  kit=["Boil water; wash hands and wounds with soap.", "Get the cowpox scratch; eat fruit and greens.", "Say no to bleeding, purging and mercury."],
                  fact="Bloodletting stayed standard medicine into the 1800s, and mercury was used against syphilis for centuries."),
    "1700s": dict(title="Stay out of the bubble", text="In 1720 all of London went mad for South Sea Company shares, and then they collapsed in months. I'd watch from the side, keep my money in dull, interest-paying government debt, and let compounding do its slow work for fifty years.",
                  kit=["Never buy at the top of a mania.", "Dull and steady beats exciting: compound interest.", "Spend on lemons, books and a good chair."],
                  fact="South Sea shares rose to around £1,000 in the summer of 1720 and fell back towards £100 by year's end; Isaac Newton reportedly lost a fortune."),
    "1800": dict(title="Store food for 1816", text="A volcano in Indonesia erupts in April 1815, and the next year has no summer: frost in June, failed harvests across Europe and New England, hunger and high prices. I'd fill the cellar in 1815, and share it.",
                 kit=["Lay in grain, salt and preserves in 1815.", "Don't plant on the old calendar in 1816.", "Keep warm clothes handy in July."],
                 fact="Mount Tambora erupted in April 1815; 1816 became the 'Year Without a Summer', with snow in New England in June."),
    "1820": dict(title="Back the railways early", text="The first real passenger railway, Liverpool to Manchester, opens in 1830 and works brilliantly. I'd put modest savings into early, sound lines, and quietly sell them all before the great railway mania of the mid-1840s collapses.",
                 kit=["Buy into early, working railways in the 1830s.", "Sell everything by 1845.", "Then take the train to the seaside."],
                 fact="The Liverpool and Manchester Railway opened in 1830; Britain's Railway Mania peaked in 1845–46 and collapsed in 1847."),
    "1840": dict(title="Sell the shovels", text="Gold is found at Sutter's Mill in January 1848. Most people who rush to California find mud and fever. The ones who do well sell them shovels, pans, boots and trousers. I'd be on the first ship, with a hold full of supplies.",
                 kit=["Be in San Francisco early in 1848.", "Sell picks, pans, boots and food; don't dig.", "Boil the water in the camps."],
                 fact="Gold was discovered on 24 January 1848; merchant Sam Brannan became one of California's first millionaires by selling supplies to miners."),
    "1860": dict(title="Keep the poison out of the house", text="The fashionable green of Victorian wallpaper, dresses and even sweets was made with arsenic. Lead was in paint and pipes, mercury in hats and medicine. A nice life starts with not slowly poisoning yourself at home.",
                 kit=["No bright green wallpaper or dyes.", "No lead paint, lead pipes or lead-glazed pots for food.", "Skip patent medicines; most were opium or alcohol."],
                 fact="Scheele's green and Paris green, both arsenic compounds, coloured wallpaper and fabrics in the 19th century."),
    "1880": dict(title="Get a safety bicycle", text="In 1885 the 'safety bicycle' arrives: two equal wheels and a chain, soon with air-filled tyres. Suddenly anyone can travel miles under their own power, for free. Exercise, fresh air and freedom, at the price of a bicycle.",
                 kit=["Buy one as soon as pneumatic tyres arrive (1888).", "Ride every day: it's exercise and escape.", "Join a cycling club; that's where the friends are."],
                 fact="The Rover safety bicycle appeared in 1885 and Dunlop's pneumatic tyre in 1888. Susan B. Anthony said in 1896 that bicycling had done more to emancipate women than anything else."),
    "1900s": dict(title="Be out of San Francisco on 18 April 1906", text="At 5:12 in the morning the ground shakes for about a minute, and then the fires burn for days. Much of the city is destroyed. I'd take a trip that week, insure everything I owned with a sound company, and come back to help rebuild.",
                  kit=["Be away from San Francisco in mid-April 1906.", "Insure your property well beforehand.", "Otherwise: a quiet life in Los Angeles, where land is still cheap."],
                  fact="The 1906 earthquake and fire destroyed much of San Francisco; around 3,000 people are estimated to have died."),
    "1910s": dict(title="Sit it out somewhere neutral", text="The decade holds a world war and then a pandemic. I'd spend it somewhere the war doesn't reach, Switzerland or a quiet farm in the Netherlands, and in the autumn of 1918 I'd stay out of crowds, keep the windows open and wait.",
                  kit=["Be in a neutral country from 1914 to 1918.", "Avoid crowds from September 1918 to early 1919.", "Write letters; keep your people close."],
                  fact="Switzerland and the Netherlands stayed neutral in the First World War; the 1918 flu peaked in many places in the autumn of 1918."),
    "1920s": dict(title="Sell before October 1929", text="The Twenties roar and the stock market climbs. Enjoy the music, by all means. But sell everything by the end of the summer of 1929, keep the cash somewhere safe, and avoid buying anything on borrowed money.",
                  kit=["Out of stocks by September 1929.", "Never buy shares on margin.", "Keep cash in more than one bank: thousands of banks will fail."],
                  fact="The Dow peaked on 3 September 1929; the crash came on 24 and 29 October 1929. Thousands of US banks failed in the years that followed."),
    "1930s": dict(title="Buy at the bottom in 1932", text="The worst of the Depression is a terrible time for most people, and a once-in-a-century chance for anyone holding cash. The market hits bottom in July 1932. I'd buy a broad slice of it then, and spend the rest helping the people around me.",
                  kit=["Buy a spread of solid companies in summer 1932.", "Hold for decades.", "Be generous: this is a hard decade for nearly everyone."],
                  fact="The Dow Jones Industrial Average bottomed at about 41 on 8 July 1932, down roughly 89% from its 1929 peak, and more than quadrupled by 1937."),
    "1940s": dict(title="Invest in friendships", text="In 1938 Harvard started following a group of young men for the rest of their lives. Its clearest finding, decades later: the quality of your close relationships predicts your health and happiness better than money or fame. In a decade of war, I'd hold on to my people.",
                  kit=["Write the letters. Keep the friends.", "Marry (or partner) for kindness.", "Join things: clubs, teams, choirs."],
                  fact="The Harvard Study of Adult Development began in 1938 and is one of the longest studies of adult life; it found close relationships are a strong predictor of health and happiness."),
    "1950s": dict(title="Learn to program", text="Computers are rare, huge and mysterious, and almost nobody knows how to tell them what to do. FORTRAN arrives in 1957. Learning it early means interesting work, good pay and a front-row seat for the most important technology of the century.",
                  kit=["Learn FORTRAN (1957), then whatever comes next.", "Work wherever the computers are.", "Keep learning: languages change, thinking doesn't."],
                  fact="IBM released FORTRAN, one of the first widely used high-level programming languages, in 1957."),
    "1960s": dict(title="Skip the tan", text="Everyone wants a deep tan, and sunbathing with baby oil is fashionable. Sunburn now means skin damage and cancer risk later. A hat, shade and long sleeves are the secret to looking and feeling well at seventy.",
                  kit=["Hats, shade and shirts in strong sun.", "Never burn; avoid the midday sun.", "And don't start smoking."],
                  fact="UV exposure and sunburn are major risk factors for skin cancers, including melanoma; the SPF rating system came into use in the 1970s."),
    "1970s": dict(title="Buy the whole market, and wait", text="In August 1976 a small company launches a strange product: a fund that simply buys the whole S&P 500 and charges almost nothing. Critics call it un-American. I'd put money into it every month and never sell.",
                  kit=["Buy the first index fund (1976).", "Add a little every month, in good years and bad.", "Keep fees low; don't try to be clever."],
                  fact="Vanguard launched the First Index Investment Trust, the first index mutual fund for ordinary investors, on 31 August 1976."),
    "1980s": dict(title="Buy into the personal computer", text="Computers are moving from basements onto desks. Microsoft goes public in March 1986 at $21 a share. A modest stake in the company that put an operating system on nearly every PC would make the rest of life comfortable.",
                  kit=["Buy a little Microsoft at its 1986 IPO, and hold it.", "Learn the PC: spreadsheets, then networks.", "Get an internet account as soon as you can."],
                  fact="Microsoft's initial public offering was on 13 March 1986 at $21 per share."),
    "1990s": dict(title="Ride the web, then step off", text="The web arrives and everything connected to it soars. Buy great companies in the mid-1990s (Apple in 1997, when it is nearly bankrupt, is the best bet of the decade), but sell the speculative ones by early 2000, before the bubble bursts.",
                  kit=["Buy Apple in 1997.", "Sell dot-com shares before March 2000.", "Build something useful on the web yourself."],
                  fact="Apple was close to bankruptcy in 1997 and received a $150 million investment from Microsoft; the NASDAQ peaked on 10 March 2000 and fell about 78% by 2002."),
    "2000s": dict(title="Mine a few bitcoin, and guard the key", text="In January 2009 a strange new kind of money appears that anyone can mine on a laptop. In 2010 someone pays 10,000 of them for two pizzas. I'd mine a few hundred, write the key on paper in two safe places, and also sell my house in 2006.",
                  kit=["Mine bitcoin in 2009; back up the key, offline, twice.", "Sell property in 2006; buy back in 2009.", "Don't take an adjustable-rate mortgage."],
                  fact="Bitcoin's first block was mined on 3 January 2009; on 22 May 2010 a programmer paid 10,000 BTC for two pizzas. US house prices peaked around 2006."),
    "2010s": dict(title="Guard your attention", text="The smartphone arrives and every app competes for your attention. A good life in the 2010s is the one where you notice that early: notifications off, the phone out of the bedroom, and evenings spent with people rather than feeds.",
                  kit=["Turn off almost every notification.", "Phone out of the bedroom.", "Read books; see friends in person."],
                  fact="Smartphone ownership rose from a minority to most adults in the US during the early 2010s; many studies link heavy social media use with poorer wellbeing, though the size of the effect is debated."),
    "2020s": dict(title="Learn to work with AI", text="In late 2022 AI becomes something anyone can talk to, and by the middle of the decade it can do real work on its own. A nice life in the 2020s belongs to people who learn early how to direct it, check it and trust it carefully.",
                  kit=["Use AI tools daily; learn what they're good and bad at.", "Learn to delegate to agents and check their work.", "Spend the time you save outdoors, with people."],
                  fact="ChatGPT launched publicly on 30 November 2022 and reached an estimated 100 million users within about two months."),
}
